00:22:13.099 --> 00:22:15.940
It's just like we have lines where we have to say.
（重复索引295的翻译）

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:22.500
But I always thought they did try to pay attention to the arc of the day in a good way.
但我一直觉得他们确实尝试以一种很好的方式去关注一天中情绪的弧光。

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:29.059
Yeah, we should also credit Michael Shea as part of designing those schedules are a wonderful AD, a PAM.
对，我们还应该把迈克尔·谢（Michael Shea）列为功臣之一，作为设计这些拍摄日程的一部分，他是一个出色的副导演（AD）、PAM。

00:22:29.059 --> 00:22:31.819
And they, 100% thought like that.
而且他们百分之百是这么想的。

00:22:31.819 --> 00:22:41.779
They thought, what's the very similitude we can create for these actors to feel like this is a real universe that they're inhabiting, they're experiencing these things kind of like they would really happen.
他们会想，我们能为这些演员创造出怎样的逼真感，让他们觉得自己身处一个真实的宇宙中，他们正在经历这些事情，就好像这些事情真的会发生一样。

00:22:41.779 --> 00:22:45.099
And I think that goes a long way to creating the vibe.
而且我认为这对于营造这种氛围大有帮助。

00:22:45.099 --> 00:22:52.420
I remember a lot of times we'd be rehearsing during the day, we'd break for dinner, let the sun go down and then shoot those nighttime scenes.
我记得很多时候，我们白天排练，晚饭时休息一下，等太阳落山后就拍摄那些夜景。

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:53.259
Yeah.
是的。

00:22:53.259 --> 00:23:05.740
Yeah, there's a rhythmic thing there's some like circadian rhythm like thing that really matters it really does help something else that matters now when i work there i just work there for a little bit in the in the in the bowels there but i saw a lot of what's going on and Craig i mean.
是的，有一种节奏感，某种类似于生物钟的东西真的很重要，它确实很有帮助。还有一件事也很重要，当我在那里工作时，虽然我只是在底层工作了一小段时间，但我看到了很多正在发生的事情，而克雷格，我是说。

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:20.759
You between you and Pam and Susie and and Michael i mean the fact that people are nice really matters it really matters to an entire work environment it's definitely something i have taken with me.
你、帕姆、苏茜还有迈克尔，我是说，大家都很友善这一点真的很重要，它对整个工作环境至关重要，这绝对是我从这段经历中学到并一直带在身上的东西。

00:23:20.759 --> 00:23:28.503
of people that I ever want to work with of just like, you might best at your job, but you just need to be nice.
我只想和这样的人共事：你也许工作能力最强，但你必须为人友善。

00:23:28.503 --> 00:23:31.084
I just want to play with nice people.
我只想和善良的人一起合作。

00:23:31.084 --> 00:23:35.339
And it really filters through the art, it shows in your days.
这种品质真的会渗透到艺术中，体现在你的每一天里。

00:23:35.339 --> 00:23:38.687
You're leaving your life, not just creating art.
你不仅是在创造艺术，你是在过你的人生。

00:23:38.687 --> 00:23:49.639
You are living your life, and being surrounded by people at the top like that who are nice, who are friendly, who are looking to be positive means something.
你正在生活，周围都是像这样顶尖、善良、友好、积极向上的人，这意味着很多。

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:51.960
There's some real misconceptions.
这里面存在一些真正的误解。

00:23:52.839 --> 00:24:06.081
Josh has wisely pointed out a few times, Josh, because you're very, you've got some great things to say about creativity in general, and the idea of sometimes that there should be an effortlessness sometimes to things.
乔什此前明智地指出了这一点好几次，乔什，因为你总是能说出一些关于创造力的深刻见解，而且有时我们会有一种想法，觉得事情有时候本该是毫不费力的。

00:24:06.081 --> 00:24:09.240
It's not always this force it into being work ethic.
工作伦理并不总是那种强求出来的状态。

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:18.992
Well, similarly, I think there's a misconception that if we're all stressed out all the time, and people are mean and people are plain scared and we're afraid to mess up.
同样，我认为也有一种误解，觉得如果我们总是处于压力之中，人们都很苛刻，甚至心怀恐惧、生怕出错。

00:24:18.992 --> 00:24:20.541
That is how we get results.
这就是我们取得成果的方式。

00:24:20.541 --> 00:24:27.024
And I think some shows and some businesses and some, well, let's just say it, American governments work that way.
我认为有些节目、有些企业，或者，我们直说吧，美国政府就是这样运作的。

00:24:27.024 --> 00:24:28.917
And I think that that's not true.
但我认为这不是真的。

00:24:28.917 --> 00:24:30.524
I think that's the huge lie.
我认为这是一个巨大的谎言。

00:24:30.524 --> 00:24:38.097
And the best place I've ever worked, the best places have always been the ones that are nice and the worst are the ones that aren't.
我呆过的最好的地方，那些最棒的地方总是那些充满善意的地方，而最糟的地方则恰恰相反。

00:24:38.097 --> 00:24:40.506
And I don't think it changes productivity.
我不认为这会改变生产力。

00:24:40.506 --> 00:24:49.628
I think you want to be, you want to have a level of respect and you want to raise your game so you're like worthy of these amazing people you get to work with.
我认为你需要保持一定程度的尊重，并且想要提升自己的水平，好让自己配得上那些和你一起工作的了不起的人。

00:24:49.628 --> 00:24:59.897
But when it doesn't come from being nice and kind and the kind of shared mission of like being excited about what you're doing, I do not think you're going to have a good product.
但如果这种动力不是源于友善、仁慈，以及对所做事情充满热情的共同使命感，我认为你根本无法做出好产品。

00:24:59.897 --> 00:25:02.536
I don't think you make good stuff out of fear.
我不认为靠恐惧能做出好东西。

00:25:02.536 --> 00:25:04.888
I'll just never be convinced out of that.
在这点上我永远不会被说服。

00:25:04.888 --> 00:25:09.879
You know, I also think that has so much to do with Pam just being the loveliest person.
你知道，我也觉得这在很大程度上是因为帕姆本来就是个极好的人。

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:35.880
Um, but also you and Carter, Craig, like I want to shout you guys out because you never, I mean, day one, season one to the final day of season nine, like there was no detectable difference in your guys' attitude around the work or your, there was no change in, I mean, you started off incredibly kind and lovely to work with and you ended that way.
嗯，还有你和卡特，克雷格，我想特此表扬你们，因为你们从来——我是说，从第一季第一天到第九季最后一天，你们对待工作的态度没有任何察觉得到的改变，你们的风格没有一丝改变——我是说，你们一开始就极其善良、非常好合作，而且直到最后也是如此。

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:49.559
And I think that in some ways, you know, we were talking about like that thing of like the ignorance is bliss, like like some ways you didn't know any better on certain levels and other things you actually intuitively understood this in a deep way.
我想从某种程度上来说，正如我们之前谈到的“无知者无畏”那种状态，某种层面上你们可能并不知道更好的方式是什么，但在另一些方面，你们其实在内心深处直观地理解了这一点。

00:25:49.559 --> 00:25:59.920
But I think like there's something really nice about you guys were just like menshey nice 29 year olds and you ended the show like menshey nice 38 year olds, 74 year olds.
我觉得特别好的一点是，你们一开始就像是那种心地善良、温和体贴的29岁年轻人，而当剧集结束时，你们就像是那种心地善良、温和体贴的38岁、74岁的成熟男人。

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:08.920
It never occurred to you guys to be tyrannical, or, you know, like my way or the highway, like there was never any of that.
你们从来没有想过去当暴君，或者说什么“不听我的就走人”，从来没有过这种心态。

00:26:08.920 --> 00:26:20.960
So I just think that it does trickle down from there, you know, most of the time, like when you hear about those toxic sets, it's generally someone pretty unhappy in like the tone setting.
所以我觉得这种风气确实是从上到下传递下来的，你知道，大多数时候，当你听到某个剧组有毒时，通常是因为定下基调的某个核心人物过得很不如意。

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:21.960
Oh, yes.
哦，是的。

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:22.960
What did I say?
我刚才说什么来着？

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:26.960
there was a bird going for something there, but did we find it?
刚才有只鸟飞过去了，不过我们找回思绪了吗？

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:29.960
Yeah, first name Lee.
对，名字叫李。

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:31.055
It's a, here we are.
就是这样，我们回来了。

00:26:31.055 --> 00:26:31.713
Here we are.
我们回来了。

00:26:31.713 --> 00:26:33.192
We're in general questions.
我们在回答常规问题。

00:26:33.192 --> 00:26:33.960
We had to try.
我们得试试。

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:34.460
Yeah.
对。

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:47.063
But you, you, you hear it's, it's, it's a person who is pretty unhappy, who, who is in the position of power and the, and the contagion of that really spreads.
但你，你，你会发现，通常是一个相当郁郁不得志的人身居高位，而这种负能量的传染性真的很强。

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.200
I 100% and I think it's that fear thing.
我百分之百赞同，我认为这就是关于恐惧的问题。

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:51.039
It's playing out of fear.
这是在出于恐惧而行事。

00:26:51.039 --> 00:26:55.599
If I make people afraid they'll do their best work, it's a lie, that will never be true.
如果我让大家感到恐惧，他们就会做出最好的工作——这是个谎言，这永远不可能是真的。

00:26:55.599 --> 00:26:58.920
And I think it's just gratitude.
而我认为这只关乎感恩。

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:05.440
I was so grateful we got to write things that an actor as fine as yourself would do.
我很感激我们能写出像你这么优秀的演员能够驾驭的角色。

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:10.960
That a producer as good as Susie would figure out how to puzzle piece to make happen that a director as good as Pam would direct.
感激像苏茜这样优秀的制片人能够像拼图一样把一切安排妥当，感激像帕姆这样优秀的导演来进行执导。

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:21.920
I never for one second didn't feel amazed and overcome with gratitude, walking into that stage and watching everybody put together these sets for ideas.
走进那个片场，看着大家把我们前一天想出的各种点子搭建成布景，我没有哪一秒钟不感到惊叹和被感恩之情淹没的。

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:26.119
We came up with the day before and they stayed up all night building that bathroom.
他们通宵达旦地搭建那个浴室。

00:27:26.119 --> 00:27:27.799
Oh, we're gonna need the bar bathroom set for this.
哦，我们需要为这个场景搭建一个酒吧浴室的布景。

00:27:27.799 --> 00:27:30.559
We had this new idea for a scene of Ted and Trudy in the bar bathroom.
我们对泰德和特鲁迪在酒吧浴室里的那场戏有了一个新点子。

00:27:30.559 --> 00:27:32.279
Let's say it's that type of thing.
比方说就是这种类型的事情。

00:27:32.279 --> 00:27:37.160
Someone stayed up late and built that set and they're gonna have to tear it down to build the next thing next day.
有人熬夜搭建了那个布景，而第二天他们又得把它拆了去建下一个场景。

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:45.039
It's just like who, I just felt such gratitude and I know Carter did too and all the writers because you just couldn't have asked for a better team.
这真的让人……我感受到了满满的感激之情，我知道卡特和所有编剧也是这么觉得的，因为你真的不可能要求更好的团队了。

00:27:45.039 --> 00:27:47.400
And I think it just all comes from gratitude.
我想这一切都源于感恩。

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:52.599
It's just all never lose sight of how amazing it is to write something.
永远不要忘记能够创作出一些东西是多么不可思议的一件事。

00:27:52.599 --> 00:27:56.400
And then 100 people, yes, end it into existence.
然后有一百个人，把它们变成现实。

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.039
That's a miracle.
这就是个奇迹。

00:27:58.039 --> 00:27:59.000
That's a miracle.
这就是个奇迹。

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:17.480
Yeah, and also, I mean, I think that the tone of the show, I mean, it sprung from you guys in your life, but also like it ultimately at the end of the day is people who really love each other and crack each other up, sitting around a bar, trying to figure out life, right?
对，而且我觉得，这部剧的基调——虽然源于你们的生活经历，但归根结底，它讲的是一群真正相爱、互相逗趣的人，围坐在酒吧里，试图参透生活，对吧？

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:24.039
And to work in an asphyxiating tense environment while you're trying to do that is just not gonna be great.
如果你试图在这种氛围下去创作，却置身于一个令人窒息的紧张环境中，那效果绝对不会好。

00:28:24.039 --> 00:28:35.640
Like, you wanna feel like, oh, we're just having some Jex mix and sitting at this bar and there's Carl and there's Wendy the waitress and like it has an ease to it, the vibe of the show.
你会希望那种感觉就像是，哦，我们只是在喝着杰克丹尼，坐在酒吧里，旁边有卡尔，还有女招待温蒂，这部剧的氛围有一种松弛感。

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:41.240
So you want the vibe in some ways to match the backstage vibe to match what's in front of the camera.
所以你会希望幕后的氛围也能与镜头前呈现的氛围相契合。

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:42.680
And I think we did that.
我想我们做到了。

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:46.240
We did, it was a thing of beauty and it doesn't always go that way.
我们做到了，这是一件美妙的事，而且事情并不总是能这么顺利。

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:51.319
And I think that energy plays into what gets on the screen every single time.
我坚信这种能量每次都会融入到最终呈现到屏幕上的画面中。

00:28:51.319 --> 00:29:11.039
All right, well, as we reach the close to the end here, I always wanna thank everyone for scooching over, making room for us in their lives and sending their questions through Instagram and everywhere, Facebook, YouTube, I'm forgetting some through our website, go to the contact page, absolutely.
好了，随着我们接近尾声，我总是想感谢大家愿意挪出时间、在生活中为我们留出空间，并通过Instagram、各种渠道、Facebook、YouTube——还有一些我可能漏掉的——通过我们的网站给我们发来问题，请务必访问联系页面。

00:29:11.039 --> 00:29:20.839
There's one question that came from the, how we made your mother Facebook groups, so not our Facebook page, but the group there, the fan group, we should go.
有一个问题来自《老爸老妈的浪漫史》Facebook群组，不是我们的官方主页，而是那个粉丝群组，我们应该去看看。

00:29:20.839 --> 00:29:23.000
Conversation's getting wild over there.
那里的讨论现在可太火热了。

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:28.640
Now she asked a great question, unfortunately, I think this is gonna drop too late for us to give her some advice.
她提了一个很棒的问题，遗憾的是，我觉得这条消息发得太晚了，我们可能来不及给她什么建议了。

00:29:28.640 --> 00:29:37.920
Maybe we could just congratulate Marissa Rosie, she says she's getting married, I think she's getting married literally seven days from when we're recording this.
也许我们可以直接祝贺玛丽莎·罗西，她说她要结婚了，我想她结婚的日子字面意义上就在我们录制这期节目的七天后。

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:39.799
This is not going to be hard.
这不难做到。

00:29:39.799 --> 00:29:41.119
Congratulations.
恭喜。

00:29:41.119 --> 00:29:42.119
Congratulations.
恭喜。

00:29:42.119 --> 00:29:43.019
I'm sure it was great.
我确信那一定很棒。

00:29:43.019 --> 00:29:44.519
Best wedding I've ever been to.
那是我去过的最好的婚礼。

00:29:44.519 --> 00:29:45.359
What?
什么？

00:29:45.359 --> 00:29:54.200
She wanted to know what's the way that she can incorporate Hymium or Hymium into her wedding to make it, to make it legendary.
她想知道她可以用什么方式将“老爸老妈的浪漫史”元素融入她的婚礼，让婚礼变得传奇。

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:55.400
Well, so again.
好吧，所以还是那句话。

00:29:55.400 --> 00:29:58.359
We'll just say congrats because we're too late.
我们只能说声恭喜，因为我们回复得太晚了。

00:29:58.359 --> 00:30:08.640
But we've seen some amazing, yeah, some yellow umbrellas, some blue French horns, some Hymium themed cakes, So people share their weddings with us all the time on social media.
不过我们确实见过一些很棒的，对吧，黄雨伞、蓝色法国号、老爸老妈主题的蛋糕，人们经常在社交媒体上与我们分享他们的婚礼。

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:18.240
And just again, that gratitude thing of like, how are we so lucky that the show can be a part of like the most important day to people share?
还是那种感恩的心情，我们会想：我们怎么会这么幸运，能让这部剧成为人们分享人生中最重要日子的一部分？

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:22.400
It's really, really humbling and thrilling.
这真的让人感到非常谦卑和激动。

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:24.279
So thank you for even thinking of that.
所以谢谢你们甚至会考虑到这一点。

00:30:24.279 --> 00:30:26.279
Sorry, we're too late with a good answer.
很抱歉我们没能及时给出好答案。

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:38.920
Well, I would say, I mean, anyone who has that kind of question, I would say, you know, You could, you could get in the weeds on like, hymium props and napkins that have whatever prop you like on them.
不过我想说，任何有这种问题的人，我想说，你可以，你可以去纠结那些细节，比如剧中的道具和印有你喜欢的道具图案的餐巾纸。

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:49.319
But I think like, if you really want to honor the spirit of the show, I think it's about being very present and alert when you're having, well, every day, every moment.
但我觉得，如果你真的想致敬这部剧的精神，我觉得关键在于在经历——嗯，每一天、每一刻时，都要保持高度的当下感和警觉性。

00:30:49.319 --> 00:31:05.400
But at these, at these crossroads moments at weddings and, you know, even funerals or engagements or first dates or eighth dates or whatever, I think it's really about showing for it and being present for it and kind of realizing that it's happening as it's happening.
但在婚礼这些人生十字路口时刻，甚至是葬礼、订婚、第一次约会或第八次约会之类的时候，我认为真正重要的是全身心投入并活在当下，并意识到它正在发生。

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:19.542
And I think that being present is the antidote to nostalgia, that if you're really present and you live strongly in the moment, you won't be pining for the past because you'll have shown up for the past.
我认为活在当下是怀旧的解药，如果你真的活在当下并强烈地体验这一刻，你就不会对过去念念不忘，因为你已经全身心投入到了那个过去中。

00:31:19.542 --> 00:31:30.410
And then, you know, and then your memories are more indelible, the more kind of awake inside of them you are, so I would just say enjoy every moment I will.
然后，你知道的，你的记忆就会更加深刻，你在其中越清醒，我就只想说：享受每一个时刻吧，我会的。

